# What git may do to a file in this tree, decided per type rather than left to
# whatever a clone happens to be configured for.
#
# Every file type this repository tracks is named below. The leg named file
# attributes in `go run ./gate` refuses a tracked file whose type is not, so a
# new kind of file arrives as a decision somebody made rather than as a default
# nobody saw.
#
# A pattern with no slash in it is what names a type. A pattern with one refines
# what a tree-wide pattern already decided and names nothing on its own.

# The default, and it decides nothing. A file reaching this line is one whose
# type has not been declared, and the gate refuses it. What the line does is
# make that refusal safe to meet: an undeclared file keeps its bytes until
# somebody chooses for it, instead of being converted while nobody is looking.
* -text

# Source, documents and configuration. Line feeds in the object and line feeds
# in the working tree on every platform, so the bytes somebody opens are the
# bytes somebody else committed and a formatter reaches the same verdict
# wherever it runs.
*.go            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.mod           text eol=lf
*.sum           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf

# Recorded bytes, and the reason this file is load bearing rather than tidy. A
# fixture is exactly what came out of a program: a column position, a trailing
# space, a carriage return that is part of the record. Converting any of it
# leaves the suite green while it reads something nobody collected. No
# conversion in either direction, and the fixture directories are named a second
# time so the rule does not depend on the extension alone.
*.fixture       -text
**/testdata/**  -text
